{"chunk_id": "6e4ba44bb04b", "text": "In this chapter, Rama hears Queen Kaikeyi's harsh demand that he relinquish the throne and live in exile for fourteen years so that Bharata may rule. Though the words strike \"like the pangs of death,\" Rama shows no anger or grief. Instead, he calmly accepts the decree, declaring that a son's highest duty is obedience to his father and the preservation of truth. He even offers to surrender not only the kingdom but all he possesses, including Sita and his own life, if it would serve his father. While King Dasharatha collapses in anguish, Rama remains serene, reverently bows to his parents, and prepares to depart. His composure, compared to a yogi and to the undiminished moon, reveals the epic's ideal of perfect dharma\u2014self-mastery, filial devotion, and unwavering righteousness even in devastating circumstances.\nRama calmly accepts Kaikeyi\u2019s demand for exile, prioritizing obedience to his father and dharma while preparing to leave Ayodhya without distress.\nThemes: filial duty, obedience to dharma, renunciation of power, exile, moral composure", "vector": [0.023602716624736786, -0.021815843880176544, -0.029950113967061043, -0.00809426512569189, -0.01701529137790203, 0.02552293799817562, -0.030563518404960632, -0.025389589369297028, -0.01621519960463047, -0.009647777304053307, 0.02364272065460682, -0.0024636168964207172, 0.00930773839354515, 0.03189700469374657, -0.012294748798012733, -0.008474308997392654, 0.05053915083408356, -0.022402578964829445, 0.029896775260567665, -0.0024002762511372566, 0.0253362488001585, -0.023082656785845757, -0.01038119476288557, 0.004647201392799616, -0.023202670738101006, -0.0006046529160812497, 0.01845545694231987, -0.028883324936032295, 0.013934937305748463, -0.02302931807935238, -0.0006459076539613307, -0.008640994317829609, 0.0011551330098882318, -0.005007243249565363, -0.021229110658168793, 0.01584182307124138, 0.002361938590183854, -0.006960801314562559, 0.0029003338422626257, -0.00